## `set-cookie` is the only header that **accumulates** instead of overwriting

In **`lib/reply.js`, lines 253–268**, `Reply.prototype.header` has a special branch just for `set-cookie`:

```js
Reply.prototype.header = function (key, value = '') {
  const _key = key.toLowerCase()

  // Special case: set-cookie accumulates into an array
  if (this[kReplyHeaders][_key] && _key === 'set-cookie') {
    if (typeof this[kReplyHeaders][_key] === 'string') {
      // First collision: upgrade scalar → array
      this[kReplyHeaders][_key] = [this[kReplyHeaders][_key]]
    }
    if (Array.isArray(value)) {
      Array.prototype.push.apply(this[kReplyHeaders][_key], value)  // spread array in
    } else {
      this[kReplyHeaders][_key].push(value)                         // append single value
    }
  } else {
    this[kReplyHeaders][_key] = value   // every other header: last write wins
  }
}
```

### What this means in practice

| Behaviour | `set-cookie` | All other headers |
|---|---|---|
| Call `.header()` twice | Values **accumulate** into an array | Second call **overwrites** the first |
| Pass an array as value | Each element pushed individually | Array stored as-is |
| Storage type | Starts as `string`, upgrades to `Array` on second write | Always whatever you passed |
| Wire format | Node's `res.setHeader('set-cookie', ['a','b','c'])` emits **three separate `Set-Cookie:` lines** | Single header line |

### Why this matters for cookies

HTTP requires each `Set-Cookie` header to be on its own line (RFC 6265). Comma-joining them (the normal multi-value approach) would corrupt cookie parsing. So when you do:

```js
reply
  .header('set-cookie', 'session=abc')
  .header('set-cookie', 'theme=dark')
  .header('set-cookie', ['a=1', 'b=2'])
```

Fastify stores `['session=abc', 'theme=dark', 'a=1', 'b=2']` and Node's `res.setHeader` emits four separate `Set-Cookie:` response lines — which is the correct behaviour per spec.

The accumulation logic is explicitly listed in the "multi-value headers" array at line 294, confirming `set-cookie` is the only header with this treatment.

